WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.380
Instructor: So in the last video,

00:01.380 --> 00:03.690
the pull request that Stevie generated

00:03.690 --> 00:05.880
was able to be automatically merged in

00:05.880 --> 00:08.370
with a single button click, right?

00:08.370 --> 00:11.370
We didn't have to do any merging locally on our machine,

00:11.370 --> 00:12.750
it was all done in GitHub,

00:12.750 --> 00:14.730
and then we just pulled down the changes.

00:14.730 --> 00:16.500
But that only happens sometimes,

00:16.500 --> 00:18.480
where the merge can be done automatically.

00:18.480 --> 00:20.430
Just like any merge in Git,

00:20.430 --> 00:23.070
there's always a potential for conflicts.

00:23.070 --> 00:24.000
So in this video,

00:24.000 --> 00:26.640
I wanna show you how you can resolve conflicts

00:26.640 --> 00:29.460
when merging a pull request, okay?

00:29.460 --> 00:32.040
So we need to first start by making a pull request

00:32.040 --> 00:34.080
that will generate a conflict.

00:34.080 --> 00:34.920
And to do that,

00:34.920 --> 00:38.850
and to do things relatively speedily, hopefully,

00:38.850 --> 00:43.850
I'm going to start by doing a tiny change as Stevie.

00:44.280 --> 00:46.650
So, here we are as Stevie.

00:46.650 --> 00:48.180
I'm going to just,

00:48.180 --> 00:50.340
I don't know, let's change the title here

00:50.340 --> 00:51.870
of the entire webpage.

00:51.870 --> 00:54.780
So we'll imagine, you know, we're doing some big task,

00:54.780 --> 00:55.920
we're working on a feature branch,

00:55.920 --> 00:58.050
but really we're just gonna change the heading.

00:58.050 --> 01:00.870
So, let's make a feature branch as Stevie.

01:00.870 --> 01:01.863
We'll call this,

01:02.700 --> 01:04.260
let me make this a bit larger,

01:04.260 --> 01:05.670
how about git, let's see,

01:05.670 --> 01:06.720
we're on main.

01:06.720 --> 01:08.970
Let's do git switch -c,

01:08.970 --> 01:10.590
because we're not gonna work on main,

01:10.590 --> 01:13.023
and then we'll call this new-heading.

01:15.060 --> 01:18.300
Okay, so switch to a new branch, new-heading.

01:18.300 --> 01:21.390
And now we can do some really basic work,

01:21.390 --> 01:24.210
like change the heading here.

01:24.210 --> 01:25.470
Where is that?

01:25.470 --> 01:29.370
Instead of an h1, I'm gonna give it a class of display-1,

01:29.370 --> 01:31.650
which is just part of Bootstrap.

01:31.650 --> 01:33.150
And let's see what it looks like.

01:33.150 --> 01:34.110
Okay, there it is.

01:34.110 --> 01:36.423
And then I'm gonna change the text to be,

01:38.280 --> 01:42.600
I don't know, "Heyo World!"

01:42.600 --> 01:44.940
Oh, how about, actually this is coming from Stevie,

01:44.940 --> 01:46.833
how about just Bock Bock.

01:48.360 --> 01:49.193
Is that how you spell that?

01:49.193 --> 01:50.026
I don't know.

01:50.026 --> 01:51.150
Whatever noise my chickens make,

01:51.150 --> 01:54.510
it's like a bock, bock, bock, bock, bock most of the time.

01:54.510 --> 01:57.180
All right, so that's Stevie's change.

01:57.180 --> 01:59.610
So, Stevie adds and commits this to the branch,

01:59.610 --> 02:01.560
so git add index,

02:01.560 --> 02:06.560
git commit -m "change heading to bock bock" (laughs softly)

02:07.170 --> 02:08.970
All right, so now we can push that up,

02:08.970 --> 02:11.010
or Stevie can push this branch,

02:11.010 --> 02:14.670
git push origin new-heading,

02:14.670 --> 02:15.503
up to GitHub.

02:16.800 --> 02:18.720
Then simultaneously,

02:18.720 --> 02:20.580
I'm not gonna go through the whole workflow

02:20.580 --> 02:22.560
of making a PR as Colt,

02:22.560 --> 02:26.250
I'm just gonna do some changes on the index

02:26.250 --> 02:28.080
directly on the main branch.

02:28.080 --> 02:30.360
Which normally, you know, we should be consistent,

02:30.360 --> 02:32.880
and if we're going to enforce pull requests,

02:32.880 --> 02:35.550
I guess I'm the the owner of this repo,

02:35.550 --> 02:37.050
so I can do whatever I want,

02:37.050 --> 02:39.000
but normally you follow some rules, right?

02:39.000 --> 02:41.490
You're not gonna have two sets of rules,

02:41.490 --> 02:45.540
you're going to ask everyone to use PRs to merge changes in.

02:45.540 --> 02:49.560
But just to demo this faster and not lose your attention,

02:49.560 --> 02:53.520
I will directly edit the file on GitHub as Colt.

02:53.520 --> 02:55.230
Right so, I can just edit a file

02:55.230 --> 02:57.990
and make a commit on the main branch.

02:57.990 --> 02:59.310
So I'm going to edit this,

02:59.310 --> 03:02.190
instead of saying hello world,

03:02.190 --> 03:03.290
where is that heading?

03:04.200 --> 03:05.220
Wait, here it is. (chuckles softly)

03:05.220 --> 03:06.180
Instead of "Hello, world,"

03:06.180 --> 03:10.143
I'll do "Hello there Everyone"

03:11.880 --> 03:14.460
and maybe a bunch of exclamation points.

03:14.460 --> 03:18.180
Okay, then I'm going to save that.

03:18.180 --> 03:19.140
Well, not really save it,

03:19.140 --> 03:21.030
but I'm going to make a new commit.

03:21.030 --> 03:22.080
So, Update index,

03:22.080 --> 03:23.280
sure, that's fine.

03:23.280 --> 03:25.290
Commit directly to the main branch.

03:25.290 --> 03:26.123
Here we go.

03:27.780 --> 03:30.480
So now the main branch has changed,

03:30.480 --> 03:33.270
and Stevie has a branch that was pushed up.

03:33.270 --> 03:35.100
This is still kind of a trivial,

03:35.100 --> 03:37.860
a silly way of creating a conflict,

03:37.860 --> 03:39.750
but there should be a conflict now,

03:39.750 --> 03:42.513
where if I try and make a pull request as Stevie,

03:43.860 --> 03:45.930
I'll take a look, here's my new heading branch.

03:45.930 --> 03:47.373
Let's make a pull request,

03:48.660 --> 03:51.030
Uh-oh, can't automatically merge.

03:51.030 --> 03:51.863
Don't worry though,

03:51.863 --> 03:53.580
you can still create the pull request.

03:53.580 --> 03:56.130
So hopefully you see why we can't automatically merge,

03:56.130 --> 03:57.150
there's a conflict.

03:57.150 --> 03:59.280
Just like if we tried to merge these on our machine

03:59.280 --> 04:02.430
with git merge, it's the exact same mechanic.

04:02.430 --> 04:03.810
So if I wanna make a pull request

04:03.810 --> 04:05.673
to change the heading to bock bock,

04:07.269 --> 04:12.269
'I am a chicken and I changed the heading to say "bock bock"

04:13.500 --> 04:15.750
I hope you like it.'

04:15.750 --> 04:16.950
Okay, so I have a message,

04:16.950 --> 04:21.543
I'll create that pull request from new-heading to main.

04:22.410 --> 04:25.680
And we see that there are conflicts that can be resolved.

04:25.680 --> 04:28.590
So the way that our repo is set up,

04:28.590 --> 04:30.510
anybody who's a collaborator currently

04:30.510 --> 04:32.130
can resolve conflicts,

04:32.130 --> 04:35.250
right, and merge or PR once that's resolved.

04:35.250 --> 04:38.430
I'm going to do it as Colt though, not as Stevie.

04:38.430 --> 04:39.690
That's a more typical workflow,

04:39.690 --> 04:41.250
is somebody submits the PR,

04:41.250 --> 04:43.080
and then it needs to be reviewed.

04:43.080 --> 04:46.920
So I'm gonna go back to Colt's workspace,

04:46.920 --> 04:49.410
and then we see there's a new PR.

04:49.410 --> 04:51.060
Here it is.

04:51.060 --> 04:52.440
And if I want to merge this in,

04:52.440 --> 04:55.800
unfortunately, it's not as simple as clicking a button.

04:55.800 --> 04:57.660
So, I can do a couple of things.

04:57.660 --> 05:00.450
If I click the Resolve conflicts button,

05:00.450 --> 05:03.840
it actually gives me an option to do this in the browser,

05:03.840 --> 05:06.750
which I haven't really done before.

05:06.750 --> 05:08.640
And this, actually for this example,

05:08.640 --> 05:11.010
is super easy to do it that way,

05:11.010 --> 05:12.330
because it's just a single line

05:12.330 --> 05:14.160
that we're keeping or combining.

05:14.160 --> 05:16.320
But rather than doing it in the browser,

05:16.320 --> 05:19.140
I'm gonna show you the good old fashioned way of doing it

05:19.140 --> 05:21.570
that you would do if you had a bunch of files.

05:21.570 --> 05:23.820
This is just one conflict in a single file,

05:23.820 --> 05:26.310
but in the real world you may run into conflicts

05:26.310 --> 05:27.780
across a bunch of files,

05:27.780 --> 05:29.850
hopefully not too many, but still,

05:29.850 --> 05:32.370
it might be easier to resolve them on your own machine.

05:32.370 --> 05:36.060
So, we can click this, "view command line instructions,"

05:36.060 --> 05:39.300
and it tells us exactly how to do this on the command line.

05:39.300 --> 05:41.700
So first, we need to bring the changes in

05:41.700 --> 05:43.350
so we can fetch origin.

05:43.350 --> 05:45.630
So, we'll do that on my machine as Colt.

05:45.630 --> 05:47.883
git fetch origin.

05:49.260 --> 05:51.660
All right, so now we have this new branch reference,

05:51.660 --> 05:52.980
this remote tracking branch.

05:52.980 --> 05:54.693
Let me make this larger again.

05:55.680 --> 05:57.330
Oh, well, that didn't work.

05:57.330 --> 05:58.830
Try that one more time.

05:58.830 --> 05:59.940
Here we are.

05:59.940 --> 06:02.400
You can see, origin/new-heading.

06:02.400 --> 06:06.150
So the instructions now tell me to check out new-heading.

06:06.150 --> 06:08.340
They're using the the checkout command

06:08.340 --> 06:10.260
on the GitHub website,

06:10.260 --> 06:11.820
they don't use git switch yet,

06:11.820 --> 06:13.470
which is the newer way of doing it.

06:13.470 --> 06:14.880
So, we could do this.

06:14.880 --> 06:17.100
We're saying "Make me a new branch called new-heading,

06:17.100 --> 06:19.470
and have it track origin/new-heading."

06:19.470 --> 06:24.120
Or the easier option is just git switch new-heading,

06:24.120 --> 06:26.880
and it will do that for us, as we've seen.

06:26.880 --> 06:28.380
So now that I've done that,

06:28.380 --> 06:32.670
I can see here, I should have "Bock Bock" showing up

06:32.670 --> 06:35.310
in the heading, and I do, I have Stevie's changes.

06:35.310 --> 06:37.410
I am currently checking out, I'm switching,

06:37.410 --> 06:38.550
or I have switched,

06:38.550 --> 06:41.640
to that new-heading branch that Stevie pushed up.

06:41.640 --> 06:44.430
So now, I need to merge in the main branch.

06:44.430 --> 06:45.840
So this approach is,

06:45.840 --> 06:48.480
switch to the new branch, the feature branch,

06:48.480 --> 06:51.210
and merge main into the feature branch,

06:51.210 --> 06:53.160
resolve the conflicts there.

06:53.160 --> 06:55.260
So, just test things out and make sure it works,

06:55.260 --> 06:58.500
and then when we're ready, we're gonna switch over to main

06:58.500 --> 07:00.093
and merge new-heading in.

07:01.350 --> 07:03.510
All right, so let's try that.

07:03.510 --> 07:05.850
git merge main.

07:05.850 --> 07:06.900
This is the main branch,

07:06.900 --> 07:09.210
we know that there's likely going to be a conflict,

07:09.210 --> 07:13.680
although I think I'm missing the latest changes from main.

07:13.680 --> 07:16.470
Yeah, it's up to date because I didn't pull down.

07:16.470 --> 07:18.330
So, let's switch to main.

07:18.330 --> 07:19.230
I'll show you what I mean,

07:19.230 --> 07:20.580
git switch main.

07:20.580 --> 07:24.960
Remember that I updated on main on GitHub,

07:24.960 --> 07:27.330
I changed the heading, and it actually tells me that,

07:27.330 --> 07:29.910
your main branch is behind origin/main.

07:29.910 --> 07:32.073
So we'll do a git pull origin main.

07:33.240 --> 07:35.340
Now we have the latest changes on main.

07:35.340 --> 07:37.533
Now I can go back to new-heading,

07:40.260 --> 07:41.970
and then I can merge main.

07:41.970 --> 07:44.430
So, git merge the new main branch that we have,

07:44.430 --> 07:47.610
it's not new, but the latest version, the latest work,

07:47.610 --> 07:49.350
merge that into this new heading branch.

07:49.350 --> 07:50.880
Now we have the conflict,

07:50.880 --> 07:53.340
so now I can resolve the conflict here.

07:53.340 --> 07:55.890
So, maybe I want both,

07:55.890 --> 08:00.690
maybe I want h1 class equals display-1 from Stevie,

08:00.690 --> 08:04.650
and then I want, I don't know, all these exclamation points?

08:04.650 --> 08:06.870
How about "Bock Bock Everyone!!!!!"

08:06.870 --> 08:07.703
Like that.

08:09.600 --> 08:12.450
So, let's see what that looks like. (chuckles)

08:12.450 --> 08:13.500
Let's refresh.

08:13.500 --> 08:14.940
Okay, great.

08:14.940 --> 08:17.130
So now we need to add and commit the changes.

08:17.130 --> 08:19.380
So git add index,

08:19.380 --> 08:22.050
and we'll just do a useless commit message,

08:22.050 --> 08:25.347
like "resolve conflicts."

08:26.520 --> 08:29.670
Okay, so now, we've merged the changes in,

08:29.670 --> 08:31.860
but we're just on the new-heading branch.

08:31.860 --> 08:33.450
So, we know for sure

08:33.450 --> 08:35.910
that the current version of master, or main,

08:35.910 --> 08:39.240
works when we merged it in with the changes on new-heading,

08:39.240 --> 08:40.650
we've resolved the conflicts,

08:40.650 --> 08:43.053
now we can switch back to main,

08:44.190 --> 08:47.130
and then we can merge in that new-heading branch

08:47.130 --> 08:49.170
that we already resolved the conflicts on,

08:49.170 --> 08:50.850
which is exactly what they ask us to do,

08:50.850 --> 08:52.800
or tell us to do, on GitHub.

08:52.800 --> 08:54.120
So, go back to main,

08:54.120 --> 08:56.580
and then merge in new-heading,

08:56.580 --> 08:58.500
which already has the conflicts resolved,

08:58.500 --> 09:00.330
so we shouldn't get new conflicts.

09:00.330 --> 09:02.640
Now this --no-ff,

09:02.640 --> 09:05.580
that's an indicator that we're telling git merge

09:05.580 --> 09:09.360
to not fast forward if it detects that it can.

09:09.360 --> 09:11.190
Because when we do a fast forward,

09:11.190 --> 09:13.380
instead of making a merge commit as we've seen,

09:13.380 --> 09:17.010
Git will just move the branch pointer to some new commit.

09:17.010 --> 09:18.330
Right? It just moves forward.

09:18.330 --> 09:20.622
And sometimes we wanna prevent that from happening,

09:20.622 --> 09:22.620
especially when we want to preserve

09:22.620 --> 09:25.350
or maintain our specific branch.

09:25.350 --> 09:27.210
We wanna make it clear that there was a branch

09:27.210 --> 09:29.250
merged in here just to have that history.

09:29.250 --> 09:30.450
You don't have to do this part,

09:30.450 --> 09:33.030
but it's just what GitHub recommends.

09:33.030 --> 09:35.700
So, we could just copy the entire line.

09:35.700 --> 09:38.340
We're merging new-heading into main.

09:38.340 --> 09:41.250
Okay, so see, I'm now gonna put a commit here

09:41.250 --> 09:42.750
instead of just a fast forward,

09:42.750 --> 09:45.030
I'm putting a a merge commit in

09:45.030 --> 09:47.760
because I said no fast forward.

09:47.760 --> 09:50.400
All right, and now we just push up to master.

09:50.400 --> 09:52.110
git push origin main.

09:52.110 --> 09:54.360
I said master again, we're using main,

09:54.360 --> 09:55.413
git push origin main.

09:59.040 --> 10:01.380
All right, so now if I refresh this page,

10:01.380 --> 10:03.990
it automatically just, I didn't refresh,

10:03.990 --> 10:07.020
but it automatically detects, successfully merged this PR,

10:07.020 --> 10:08.220
and it's closed now,

10:08.220 --> 10:10.890
and you can delete that new-heading branch if you want.

10:10.890 --> 10:12.333
Sure, let's delete it.

10:13.650 --> 10:15.630
We can also restore it if needed.

10:15.630 --> 10:19.860
And now we merged the PR in that had a conflict.

10:19.860 --> 10:22.560
And the same process would apply if there was one conflict

10:22.560 --> 10:24.750
or tons and tons of conflicts,

10:24.750 --> 10:27.090
we make a pull request, and then resolve the conflicts,

10:27.090 --> 10:28.740
following the instructions.

10:28.740 --> 10:31.800
Basically, pull that code down, or fetch it,

10:31.800 --> 10:34.620
check it out, or switch to the new feature branch,

10:34.620 --> 10:36.870
merge master or main in,

10:36.870 --> 10:37.830
resolve conflicts,

10:37.830 --> 10:38.700
add and commit,

10:38.700 --> 10:40.710
then switch back to master or main,

10:40.710 --> 10:44.040
merge in the now conflictless branch,

10:44.040 --> 10:46.170
and then push that up.

10:46.170 --> 10:49.500
Okay, so now the last thing to do is, as Stevie,

10:49.500 --> 10:52.260
go and get the latest version, right, of the main branch,

10:52.260 --> 10:54.210
we don't have that as Stevie.

10:54.210 --> 10:55.410
So we just need to pull down,

10:55.410 --> 10:57.180
we'll switch to main.

10:57.180 --> 11:00.810
We can remove that branch now, new-heading,

11:00.810 --> 11:04.503
so git branch -D new-heading.

11:05.820 --> 11:08.580
And now, git pull origin main,

11:08.580 --> 11:10.860
we'll get the latest changes from main.

11:10.860 --> 11:12.540
And there's not a whole lot that we got,

11:12.540 --> 11:14.610
but if we type git log, we see,

11:14.610 --> 11:17.670
okay, we got the resolving of the conflict,

11:17.670 --> 11:20.130
merged that branch in, my work was merged in.

11:20.130 --> 11:23.400
So now, our latest version of the main branch

11:23.400 --> 11:25.260
says "Bock Bock Everyone!!!!!" (laughs softly)

11:25.260 --> 11:28.110
And we can take a look at it right here.

11:28.110 --> 11:29.160
And there we are,

11:29.160 --> 11:32.069
so we successfully merged a PR in that had a conflict.

11:32.069 --> 11:33.093
Okay.
